The mechanism is exactly what `Audio` and the `ViewBuilder` show. Here's the analysis and plan.

## How to prevent constructor use (answer)

Two complementary levers:

**1. The library's own convention — make the constructor `private`.** Java can only prevent `new` if there is no accessible constructor; the library views do exactly this (`Audio`/`Text` have `private` constructors + a static `with(Consumer<Config>)` returning `ViewBuilder`). Any custom view can adopt it:

```java
public final class PlayerBar implements View, Configurable<PlayerBar.Config> {
    public static final class Config implements View.Config {
        private WritableSignal<Integer> trackIndex;
        private WritableSignal<Float> position;
        // ... volume, playing, seekRequest, volumeRequest

        public Config trackIndex(WritableSignal<Integer> v) { this.trackIndex = v; return this; }
        // ...
    }
    private final Config config;
    private PlayerBar(Config config) { this.config = config; }          // ← private: no `new PlayerBar(...)`

    public static ViewBuilder<PlayerBar, Config> with(Consumer<Config> configure) {
        Config config = new Config();
        configure.accept(config);
        return new ViewBuilder<>(new PlayerBar(config));
    }
    @Override public Config config() { return config; }
    @Override public View body() { /* reads config.trackIndex … */ }
}
```

`ViewBuilder` requires `V extends View & Configurable<C>` + `config()`, so this is the full pattern. Call sites change `new PlayerBar(a, b, c, …)` → `PlayerBar.with(p -> p.trackIndex(…).position(…).playing(…).volume(…).seekRequest(…).volumeRequest(…))`.

**2. Tooling enforcement (optional, stronger).** `PersistedProcessor` already walks every class implementing `View` (`@SupportedAnnotationTypes("*")` + `View` erasure check). It could be extended to emit a **compile error** when a `View` implementation exposes a non-private constructor — forcing the `with(Consumer<Config>)` configurator on all app-authored views. This is a policy decision (see questions), and the demo would need all its parameterized custom views converted to comply.

## Plan: convert `PlayerBar` to the configurator

1. **`PlayerBar.java`**: add `Configurable<PlayerBar.Config>` + nested `Config` (the 6 writable-signal fields with chainable setters), `private` ctor, static `with(...)` → `ViewBuilder`, `config()`, `body()` reads from `config`.
2. **`MusicPlayerView.java`** (line ~97): `new PlayerBar(...)` → `PlayerBar.with(p -> p.…(…))`.
3. **Tests/compile**: `mvn -o -q -pl pathland-demo-views -am test` — 31 green; full reactor sanity.
4. **Docs**: note the private-constructor convention in `AGENTS.md` custom-view section (one line).

Optional same-treatment candidates (currently `new`-constructed): `NowPlayingSidebar(trackIndex)`, `LibraryView(trackIndex, position, playing, seekRequest)`, `TrackRowView(track, index, +4 signals)`, `SectionCard(title, content)`. `MusicPlayerView` (State fields, no args) and `PlayerControlsStyle` (a Style, built via its own `of(...)` factory — not a `View`) would stay as-is.

## Questions before I execute

1. **Scope** — only `PlayerBar`, or also the other parameterized custom views (`LibraryView`, `TrackRowView`, `NowPlayingSidebar`, `SectionCard`)?
2. **Enforcement** — (a) convention only: private ctor + `with(...)`, rely on code review; or (b) also extend `PersistedProcessor` to *error* on non-private `View` constructors (turning the demo's remaining `new`-views into compile failures)? Option (b) is stronger but a real API/strictness change affecting every app's custom views.
3. Since `with(...)` on a config takes signal types directly, should each `Config` setter also keep a **static rawer overload** where it makes sense (e.g. none here — these are bindings, not literals), matching the library's raw+signal rule? My read: these stay writable-signal-only, like `Audio.Config`.

How would you like to proceed?

